Implemented `transpose` in `transpose.py:1`.

How it works: splits input on newlines, then for each column collects that column's characters top-to-bottom; positions beyond a row's length become `None` (left/missing padding). Trailing `None` entries are dropped (no right padding), while genuine spaces are preserved. Finally it joins rows with newlines.

Verified by running all 12 official Exercism canonical cases (empty, single line, ragged/mixed lengths, triangle, jagged triangle, square, rectangle) — all passed — plus `py_compile` for syntax. No test files were created or modified.
